<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Two forks decide which Whirlpool code you're reading: top-load or front-load, and old code or new code.</h2>

    <p><strong>Whirlpool washers split two ways that change the diagnosis. First, top-load (Cabrio, Bravos) versus front-load (Duet, Maxima): the same code number can point to different hardware, because a front-loader has a drain pump filter and a top-loader doesn't. Second, code generation: around 2011 Whirlpool moved from two-part codes (<span class="code">F21</span>, <span class="code">F51</span>, <span class="code">F02</span>) to the <span class="code">FxEy</span> format (<span class="code">F9E1</span>, <span class="code">F7E1</span>). Same faults, renamed. Below we split the tables by machine type and spell out the old-to-new translations. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>Most Whirlpool washer codes fall into four families no matter which machine you have: a drain fault (F9E1, old F21), a fill fault (F8E1, old LF), a lock fault (F5E2, plus the lid variants F5E1/F5E3), and a motor or basket-speed fault (F7E1, old F51). Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair decode the exact code, confirm on-site, and — critically — bring parts for the right machine type, because sending a front-load filter to a top-load Cabrio call helps nobody.</p>

    <p>Whirlpool, Maytag, and KitchenAid share this whole system. Read on for the top-load table, the front-load table, and the generation-transition key. Parent reference: <a href="/services/washer-repair/error-codes/">all washer error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/whirlpool-washer-repair/">Whirlpool washer repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Top-load</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Cabrio &amp; Bravos (high-efficiency top-load) codes.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Lid on top, no front door, no front filter. On these machines a drain code goes straight to the pump and hose, and the lock hardware is a lid lock, not a door interlock.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>F5E1</td><td>Lid switch — reads open when closed</td><td>Lid switch or wiring</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F5E2</td><td>Lid won't lock</td><td>Lid lock assembly ($260-440)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F5E3</td><td>Lid won't unlock at cycle end</td><td>Lid lock assembly stuck</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F7E1</td><td>Motor / basket speed fault</td><td>Rotor position sensor, shifter, drive motor (old F51)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F8E1 (LF)</td><td>Long fill / no water</td><td>Inlet valve, hose, screen, water pressure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F9E1 (Ld)</td><td>Long drain / drain fault</td><td>Drain pump, hose (no front filter on top-load)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>uL / Ub</td><td>Unbalanced load</td><td>Redistribute; suspension if persistent</td></tr>
        <tr><td>oL</td><td>Overloaded</td><td>Too much laundry — remove some</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sd / Sud</td><td>Excess suds</td><td>Too much or non-HE detergent (customer-side)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F1 / F1E1</td><td>Main control fault</td><td>Control board (technician)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Front-load</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Duet &amp; Maxima (front-load) codes.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Round door, drain pump filter behind the lower kickplate. On these the very first stop for a drain code is that filter — it clears roughly half of drain faults without a part.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>F5E2</td><td>Door lock fault</td><td>Door interlock assembly ($260-440)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F5E3</td><td>Door won't unlock</td><td>Interlock stuck</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F7E1</td><td>Motor / basket speed fault</td><td>Motor or motor control (old F51)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F8E1 (LF)</td><td>Long fill / no water</td><td>Inlet valve, hose, screen, pressure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F8E2</td><td>Dispenser / no water detected</td><td>Dispenser motor or clog</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F9E1 (Ld)</td><td>Long drain / drain fault</td><td>Filter first, then hose, then pump (old F21/F02)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sud / F30</td><td>Excess suds</td><td>Detergent dose / non-HE soap (customer-side)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Related symptom pages: <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a> · <a href="/services/washer-repair/wont-spin/">washer won't spin</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Generation transitions</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Old two-part codes → new FxEy codes.</h2>
    <p class="lead">If you're reading a pre-2011 manual against a newer washer (or the reverse), translate the code first. The fault didn't change — the label did.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Old code (pre-~2011)</th><th>New code</th><th>Same fault</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>F21</td><td>F9E1</td><td>Long drain / drain fault</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F02</td><td>F9E1</td><td>Long drain (front-load, suds-related)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F51</td><td>F7E1</td><td>Motor rotor position / basket speed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LF</td><td>F8E1</td><td>Long fill / water supply</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ld</td><td>F9E1</td><td>Long drain (alt display)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sud</td><td>F30 / Sud</td><td>Excess suds</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>So an old Duet flashing F21 and a new one flashing F9E1 have the identical drain problem, and the fix is the same drain path: filter (front-load), hose, then pump at $380 to $540.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Shared platform</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool, Maytag, and KitchenAid are one system.</h2>
    <p>These three brands share control architecture. Maytag's Bravos is the Cabrio; Maytag's Maxima is the Duet. F9E1 means long-drain on all three, F5E2 means lock fault on all three, and the parts come from the same catalog families. Our techs treat them as a single platform, which means the diagnostic and the part numbers carry straight across.</p>
    <p>Brand-specific pages: <a href="/services/washer-repair/maytag-error-codes/">Maytag washer error codes</a> · <a href="/brands/whirlpool-washer-repair/">Whirlpool washer repair</a> · <a href="/brands/maytag-washer-repair/">Maytag washer repair</a> · KitchenAid washer repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY resets</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug for 60 seconds (or flip the breaker), restore power, run a rinse-and-spin. Clears transient sensor noise.</li>
      <li><strong>Cancel twice.</strong> On many models, press Cancel/Pause or Stop/Drain twice to clear a latched code.</li>
      <li><strong>Drain pump filter (front-load Duet/Maxima).</strong> Lower kickplate access, pan underneath, clear debris. Fixes a high share of F9E1 drain codes.</li>
      <li><strong>Inlet hose &amp; screen (F8E1).</strong> Valves open, hoses not kinked, mesh screens clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Detergent dose (Sud).</strong> Cut the amount, use HE detergent.</li>
      <li><strong>Reduce load (oL / uL).</strong> Overload and unbalance clear when you spread or remove laundry.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Lock faults (F5E2/F5E3) and motor faults (F7E1) don't clear from a reset when the hardware's actually down — those are the ones worth a call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A year 8+ Cabrio or Duet throwing several codes usually has a dying board.</h2>
    <p>When an older Whirlpool shows a drain code one week, an unbalance code the next, and a lock code after that, the honest read is the main control board aging out and sending false signals — not three separate broken parts. Year 8 to 12 is the typical window. Board replacement runs $580 to $880, and on a mid-tier machine that age it's a repair-versus-replace call. We run that math with you at the door instead of quietly installing a board on a washer that's near the end.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Code-driven part costs.</h2>
    <p class="lead">$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. No emergency surcharge — same $89 anytime.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code-driven repair</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
